now back to our breaking news a former  soldier who claims that his hands were  wet and that his gun slipped when he  shot and killed a man in 1988 has been  found guilty of manslaughter David  Holden shot Aiden mcanespie at a county  Tyrone border checkpoint in Northern  Ireland during the troubles well let's  bring in our senior Island correspondent  David Blevins who's in Belfast for us  this ruling's been a long time coming  David tell us more  yes David Holden was an 18 year old  member of the Grenadier guards on duty  at okner cloy in County Tyrone when 23  year old Aiden magnesby was fatally shot  in the back after passing through a  security checkpoint in 1998. Mr Holden  who's Now 52 admitted that the shots had  been discharged from a machine gun in  his hands but he said it had been fired  by accident that his hands were wet and  that his finger had only been on the  trigger for a number of seconds at the  court during this trial heard that he  had previously identified Mr mconesby as  a person of interest to the security  forces and therefore the prosecution  made the point that of all the places  where those shots could have ricocheted  it was quite unbelievable that they  could by accident have struck the very  person who was under surveillance by Mr  Holden at the time some really damning  words from the judge in the last half  hour Mr Justice O'Hara said the evidence  that Mr Holden gave during his trial was  entirely unconvincing that it was  incoherent that he did not believe him  that he had created a false account of  what happened and he said he was beyond  any reasonable doubt criminally culpable  and found him guilty of manslaughter he  is the first former British soldier to  have been convicted of a historical  offense in Northern Ireland since the  1998 Good Friday agreement six veterans  to date have been charged two of those  cases collapsed another two were halted  and in the case of one soldier he died  while on trial so this really is a  significant finding and of course it  comes at the time when there is much  debate about the government's attempt to  bring forward legislation to effectively  end the prosecution of soldiers for  historical offenses in Northern Ireland  victims on all sides have fiercely  opposed that legislation because of  course it doesn't just grant the fact to  amnesty to former soldiers but to former  terrorists and the government just  yesterday said it would bring forward  amendments to address some of those  concerns but they're still not  acceptable to victims so this former  Soldier has been found guilty of  manslaughter back in 1990 and back in  1988 and he will come before the court  again in a few weeks time for sentencing
 

